Yannis exhales through his nose, a laugh caught between teeth. “Fine, no Vienna; I’ll route the piano through a bit-crusher and call it ‘emotion.’”

She smiles, small and sharp. “You keep saying that; it’s your whole brand.”

“Your whole brand is looping things into oblivion until they start to breathe.” He nods toward the dim hall. “Come in; kettle’s on; I’ll show you the chain;

“I don’t need a metronome,” he said. “I have you. You have the best heart I’ve ever heard in a metronome.”

She flushed, then forced herself to inhale. “Okay. Four beats. I count. You listen. You step.”

He nodded. The rehearsal room smelled of camphor and old paper. Odelia set the recorder on the floor and lifted her hands. She didn’t count with her mouth;

Meera’s mouth quirked. “Noted. I’ll double-check the receiver filters in the XML. If you see the ACK in logcat but the UI doesn’t update, I’ll flag the dispatcher thread.”

“Cool. I’ll keep the sim looping. If we get a clean run with ACK, I’ll post the fix summary in #dev-ops and loop the QA lead.”

“Sounds good. I’ll also validate the notification payload size;

He grinned. “Then waxed brass pins and a thin wooden stop. Or skip the doors entirely: paint the fence, and call it a day.”

Willow turned her sketchbook toward him, pencil poised. “I need to explain the science of fence paint in terms of fiction. Give me a metaphor that makes sense to a librarian.”

Uche tapped the rail. “Fences are like sentences: they keep the chaos out. Paint is just the ink;

“Tighten until snug; don’t overtighten. A thin strip of rubber sheet between the door and the frame eliminates squeak. Or a drop of sewing oil in the pin—go for least expensive, but pick one you can live with.”

She nodded and scribbled. “Okay. I’ll do the pin, the rubber, and the oil. I need to stop.”

